From: Bryan Hoban <bryan.hoban@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/i915: Standard Dolby Vision through an onboard DP-to-HDMI converter

Some gen11+ boards have no native HDMI: their HDMI sockets are DisplayPort
outputs behind an onboard DP-to-HDMI protocol converter (PCON). Such a
converter already turns the DP InfoFrame SDP in the GMP DIP into the HDMI
DRM InfoFrame for HDR10. It does the same for the Dolby VSIF.

On ports where it is allowed, the DP connector gets DVBRIDGE_DV_STANDARD_LAB.
With it set, compute_config checks the converter route (branch device, DFP
type HDMI, HDMI sink), validates the link with the shared policy
(verified_bridge in place of native_hdmi), and computes the DV AVI (CTA VIC,
RGB, full range, no colorimetry) and the Dolby VSIF (signal = 1). The VSIF
is sent as a DP InfoFrame SDP in the GMP DIP, the AVI as one in the AVI DIP.
The VS DIP is not used: it blanks the verified converter's output. The
compute_formats() retry without the downstream TMDS limit is skipped for DV.
Readout maps both SDPs back to HDMI InfoFrames for the state checker.

Allowed: boards in a DMI allowlist (INUSUAL INUC 11i5FL, board CM11EBV58W),
or any gen11+ HDMI-capable non-LSPCON DP port with i915.dv_pcon=1
(experimental). i915.dv_pcon=0 turns it off. Not on LSPCON ports or eDP.

Verified: INUC 11i5FL (Tiger Lake), both HDMI sockets, Sony Bravia 7
(TV-led DV, VSVDB v2), 4K23.976 and 4K59.94 with an RGB8 tunnel test frame,
and Kodi playback of Dolby Vision titles (LibreELEC-yblod 0.3.4-test1).

